00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:49,950
and how do we create room for everyone

263
00:12:49,950 --> 00:12:51,870
to join the discussion.

264
00:12:51,870 --> 00:12:55,830
As someone who's taught for 25 years, in my own syllabus,

265
00:12:55,830 --> 00:12:57,927
which I loved the skit today that was like

266
00:12:57,927 --> 00:12:59,520
♪ It's in the syllabus ♪

267
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:01,227
- That was the best.

268
00:13:01,227 --> 00:13:02,820
- But it says specifically,

269
00:13:02,820 --> 00:13:04,830
because my class has been called

270
00:13:04,830 --> 00:13:06,300
How to Learn with your Mobile Device.

271
00:13:06,300 --> 00:13:08,010
So, I'm teaching students how to learn

272
00:13:08,010 --> 00:13:10,710
and how to use technology for learning purposes.

273
00:13:10,710 --> 00:13:12,530
And I say we're gonna talk about using AI

274
00:13:12,530 --> 00:13:15,240
as a learning tool, but we're also gonna talk about

275
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:18,960
the difference between using it and abusing it, right?

276
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:22,980
And so I mentioned specifically that if you use AI

277
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:25,410
to write your papers, you're giving away your voice.

278
00:13:25,410 --> 00:13:26,640
And for some of you,

279
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:27,840
that may make you sound more

280
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:30,210
like a white male than you want.

281
00:13:30,210 --> 00:13:33,960
And so it's very important for students to be aware

282
00:13:33,960 --> 00:13:37,230
of kind of not only the ramifications

283
00:13:37,230 --> 00:13:40,890
of academic integrity and all that,

284
00:13:40,890 --> 00:13:44,010
but also that we want their original work.

285
00:13:44,010 --> 00:13:46,140
We want their voices to be heard

286
00:13:46,140 --> 00:13:50,580
because that's how we make a better society, et cetera.

287
00:13:50,580 --> 00:13:54,630
So, the conversations that Lynn kind of provoked

288
00:13:54,630 --> 00:13:56,103
with this prompt here,

289
00:13:56,970 --> 00:13:59,820
we're gonna have different ones with different stakeholders.

290
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,980
The administrators are going to be like,

291
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:03,210
how do we stay ahead of this?

292
00:14:03,210 --> 00:14:04,770
For the students, it's like,

293
00:14:04,770 --> 00:14:06,030
we know you're already doing it.

294
00:14:06,030 --> 00:14:07,470
And then sometimes you might be doing it

295
00:14:07,470 --> 00:14:09,690
even if the instructor asks you not to.

296
00:14:09,690 --> 00:14:14,250
And in some cases it might be questionable.

297
00:14:14,250 --> 00:14:16,800
So, and again, in my syllabus, I say,

298
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:18,990
before you ask AI to write your paper,

299
00:14:18,990 --> 00:14:23,070
ask me the teacher for an extension and see how that works.

300
00:14:23,070 --> 00:14:27,750
And so when we start engaging people on our campuses

301
00:14:27,750 --> 00:14:30,840
and our districts and throughout the consortial regions

302
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:33,094
in conversations around AI,

303
00:14:33,094 --> 00:14:35,310
let's be active listeners,

304
00:14:35,310 --> 00:14:38,490
let's hear what the challenges people are that they perceive

305
00:14:38,490 --> 00:14:40,200
or are actually feeling,

306
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:42,720
and then figure out ways that we can work together

307
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:44,463
to keep moving forward.

308
00:14:45,810 --> 00:14:48,270
- Thank you, Kevin. Did you have more to add?

309
00:14:48,270 --> 00:14:50,040
- I'd like to respond to a couple things

310
00:14:50,040 --> 00:14:52,390
that Kevin brought up and

311
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:59,550
the other acronym that's the big acronym in the AI space

312
00:14:59,550 --> 00:15:01,290
and kind of AI policy and guidelines

313
00:15:01,290 --> 00:15:03,060
is the HUMANS Principles.

314
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:05,190
And we do have a T-shirt

315
00:15:05,190 --> 00:15:07,200
with the HUMANS Principles on it now.

316
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:11,280
So, I should, yeah, kind of get the website out to y'all

317
00:15:11,280 --> 00:15:13,410
because I was wearing it at the AI Fellows Retreat

318
00:15:13,410 --> 00:15:14,728
on Monday.

319
00:15:14,728 --> 00:15:17,697
And it's great, it's another acronym,

320
00:15:17,697 --> 00:15:22,200
and the U in HUMAN stands for kind of universal access

321
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:23,640
and universal design.

322
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:26,700
And that gets to the point that we should not be amplifying

323
00:15:26,700 --> 00:15:30,840
the digital divide through the use of AI.

324
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:33,870
And then that directly tracks to the announcement

325
00:15:33,870 --> 00:15:37,050
that Google made this morning about the MOU signing

326
00:15:37,050 --> 00:15:39,180
and the availability of premium level tools

327
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:41,280
for all of our students in the system,

328
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:44,100
which is something the Chancellor very much wants.

329
00:15:44,100 --> 00:15:46,260
Now of course, that has to get turned on

330
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:48,390
district by district and college by college,

331
00:15:48,390 --> 00:15:51,930
which means having the conversation locally about,

332
00:15:51,930 --> 00:15:54,840
you know, developing AI literacy at your campus.

333
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:57,690
So, are you ready to turn on those functions?

334
00:15:57,690 --> 00:16:00,720
Is this something that there is agreement on?

335
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:03,750
And kind of what are the step-by-step processes

336
00:16:03,750 --> 00:16:08,428
to get to a place where that makes sense to do locally,

337
00:16:08,428 --> 00:16:09,960
under local control?

338
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:11,970
But that option is now available,

339
00:16:11,970 --> 00:16:13,380
which is incredibly exciting.

340
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:16,200
And so we'll see, you know, how that starts to play out

341
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:19,680
at different campuses around the state.

342
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:22,650
So, just wanted to kind of add that on.

343
00:16:22,650 --> 00:16:23,783
- Yeah, thank you,

344
00:16:23,783 --> 00:16:28,230
because that leads right into the next query, which is,

345
00:16:28,230 --> 00:16:30,510
you know, it's very clear that we're committed

346
00:16:30,510 --> 00:16:33,067
to developing AI literacy, at least all of us up here.

347
00:16:33,067 --> 00:16:35,580
You know, like AI literacy is important,

348
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:39,450
especially AI literacy around using it effectively,

349
00:16:39,450 --> 00:16:43,830
appropriately, ethically, and transparently.

350
00:16:43,830 --> 00:16:47,220
And that goes for faculty and for students.

351
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:51,780
And the question then becomes, that's the what,

352
00:16:51,780 --> 00:16:54,098
the question then becomes, how?

353
00:16:54,098 --> 00:16:57,090
How do we do that?

354
00:16:57,090 --> 00:16:59,343
So, do you guys know?

355
00:17:00,990 --> 00:17:03,690
- I have only personal approaches to offer here.

356
00:17:03,690 --> 00:17:05,760
So, I like the acronym focus here.

357
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:08,610
So I'll share one more that I came up with from my class

358
00:17:08,610 --> 00:17:11,100
and how I approach AI in that room.

359
00:17:11,100 --> 00:17:12,720
The first thing was obviously having

360
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:13,880
an AI policy on the syllabus

361
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:15,510
so the students understood that in my class

362
00:17:15,510 --> 00:17:17,220
they would be exposed to it.

363
00:17:17,220 --> 00:17:20,430
They would be using ChatGPT, Midjourney, a few other tools

364
00:17:20,430 --> 00:17:22,920
that we were using for graphics, artwork, et cetera.

365
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:26,550
And I give them prompts to use. So, that's just upfront.

366
00:17:26,550 --> 00:17:29,070
Then I created this thing that I was like,

367
00:17:29,070 --> 00:17:30,000
acronyms, right?

368
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,920
TRUST, right? We love this word.

369
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:34,200
And so I said, well, you just mentioned transparency.

370
00:17:34,200 --> 00:17:36,390
So, that's actually what the first T stands for.

371
00:17:36,390 --> 00:17:39,090
So it's use AI with transparency.

372
00:17:39,090 --> 00:17:41,613
No hiding it. On my end either, by the way.

373
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:44,670
Responsibility, are you using it responsibly?

374
00:17:44,670 --> 00:17:45,540
What does that even mean?

375
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:47,730
A conversation that we've been having here at this summit.

376
00:17:47,730 --> 00:17:51,360
It's absolutely clear that there is levels of usage,

377
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:53,700
but we determine to be responsible or irresponsible.

378
00:17:53,700 --> 00:17:56,160
So, whatever you determine in your classroom

379
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:58,320
as part of your academic freedom,

380
00:17:58,320 --> 00:18:01,560
you can make that much AI be part of the class.

381
00:18:01,560 --> 00:18:03,720
Use it responsibly based on these guidelines.

382
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:06,240
We also have statewide and national guidelines.

383
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:08,790
I mean, AI is really a global conversation,

384
00:18:08,790 --> 00:18:11,610
so we should abide by the statewide guidelines, certainly,

385
00:18:11,610 --> 00:18:13,080
but it is a global conversation.

386
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:14,280
So seeing that others,

387
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:15,990
other countries, other states,

388
00:18:15,990 --> 00:18:19,170
other parts of the world are looking at this technology too.

389
00:18:19,170 --> 00:18:20,250
You get a bigger impression on,

390
00:18:20,250 --> 00:18:23,550
well, what is this gonna look like in a global sense?

391
00:18:23,550 --> 00:18:26,250
So, use it transparently, use it responsibly,

392
00:18:26,250 --> 00:18:28,050
use it with understanding.

393
00:18:28,050 --> 00:18:30,510
That implies misusage.

394
00:18:30,510 --> 00:18:31,890
That implies you play a little bit

395
00:18:31,890 --> 00:18:32,910
to make some mistakes, right?

396
00:18:32,910 --> 00:18:34,800
One of my favorite teachers is Ms. Frizzle.

397
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:36,510
Take chances, make mistakes, get messy.

398
00:18:36,510 --> 00:18:37,380
Absolutely.

399
00:18:37,380 --> 00:18:39,210
So, use it with understanding of when you should

400
00:18:39,210 --> 00:18:40,350
and when you shouldn't.

401
00:18:40,350 --> 00:18:42,030
That's part of AI literacy too.

402
00:18:42,030 --> 00:18:43,560
Digital literacy is what it was called earlier.

403
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:45,030
I agree with that.

404
00:18:45,030 --> 00:18:46,173
Use it with strategy.

405
00:18:47,010 --> 00:18:49,140
Again, that requires some higher order thinking here.

406
00:18:49,140 --> 00:18:52,770
I hear a lot of talk of AI diminishing critical thinking.

407
00:18:52,770 --> 00:18:55,050
I'm like, I don't think that that has to be the case.

408
00:18:55,050 --> 00:18:57,270
There are strategies and approaches we could use

409
00:18:57,270 --> 00:19:00,540
to mitigate that, to have them use AI more strategically.

410
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:02,790
And then the final one is tenacity.

411
00:19:02,790 --> 00:19:05,610
And I like this word because it's this grit focused word.

412
00:19:05,610 --> 00:19:07,770
Be tenacious, don't give up, don't quit.

413
00:19:07,770 --> 00:19:08,730
Try again.

414
00:19:08,730 --> 00:19:10,710
One of my first policies in my syllabus

415
00:19:10,710 --> 00:19:13,320
is that weak prompts will make for weak outputs.

416
00:19:13,320 --> 00:19:15,600
So, we've all heard here that prompting is good to learn.

417
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:16,890
It's part of this new literacy

418
00:19:16,890 --> 00:19:20,280
that's now being required by jobs, et cetera.

419
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:23,460
So, I would say trust your students, lead with trust,

420
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:26,814
but it's not trust that comes just with nothing there.

421
00:19:26,814 --> 00:19:29,070
It's like, I gave you the tools, I gave you guidelines,

422
00:19:29,070 --> 00:19:33,120
I gave you frameworks, I gave you transparency and trust.

423
00:19:33,120 --> 00:19:35,610
And I lead with that, because I hear too many conversations

424
00:19:35,610 --> 00:19:39,780
here about over-policing students and unethical usage

425
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:41,910
is always the default conversation.

426
00:19:41,910 --> 00:19:44,160
I'm like, we can have a far more critical,

427
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:46,800
higher order thinking conversation too.

428
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:49,320
- Thank you. I totally agree with that.

429
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:51,570
The culture of trying to catch them

430
00:19:51,570 --> 00:19:54,420
for doing something wrong

431
00:19:54,420 --> 00:19:59,420
is just not the best, healthiest classroom feel, you know?

432
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:02,970
Craig, did you have something to add to this?

433
00:20:02,970 --> 00:20:05,100
- Yeah, there are a lot of false positives

434
00:20:05,100 --> 00:20:07,230
and false negatives with that approach,

435
00:20:07,230 --> 00:20:11,179
and of trying to catch students out

436
00:20:11,179 --> 00:20:15,180
and kind of just block AI entirely.

437
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:18,150
I mean, that sort of genie is out of the bottle, right?

438
00:20:18,150 --> 00:20:21,330
And depending which survey you look at,

439
00:20:21,330 --> 00:20:23,820
you'll see different levels of AI usage,

440
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:26,420
but we all know it's quite prevalent among students.

441
00:20:27,570 --> 00:20:31,920
And so Rodrigo, let me ask you,

442
00:20:31,920 --> 00:20:34,260
is the TRUST acronym,

443
00:20:34,260 --> 00:20:36,690
are you including that in your syllabus, or is that,

444
00:20:36,690 --> 00:20:39,390
yeah, so that would be one approach I think

445
00:20:39,390 --> 00:20:40,890
that would be wonderful

446
00:20:40,890 --> 00:20:43,470
if we could just kind of universally understand

447
00:20:43,470 --> 00:20:48,060
that students need to know about your position

448
00:20:48,060 --> 00:20:51,840
in your classroom on the use of AI.

449
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:54,960
And the Academic Senate for California Community Colleges

450
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:59,340
put out a terrific paper called Academic Integrity

451
00:20:59,340 --> 00:21:01,560
in the Era of Artificial Intelligence.

452
00:21:01,560 --> 00:21:06,060
And in that document, they advocate for including

453
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:09,510
like sort of a meter or a dial, right,

454
00:21:09,510 --> 00:21:13,110
from wide open, you know, go for it,

455
00:21:13,110 --> 00:21:14,790
use AI for anything,

456
00:21:14,790 --> 00:21:17,130
to it's okay within certain parameters,

457
00:21:17,130 --> 00:21:18,750
and we're gonna discuss what those parameters are,

458
00:21:18,750 --> 00:21:21,240
to, you know, this is a closed,

459
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:23,760
there's no AI permitted in this class.

460
00:21:23,760 --> 00:21:27,123
And that's fine, right? Because that's academic freedom.

461
00:21:28,140 --> 00:21:30,423
And I think, you know, most,

462
00:21:32,190 --> 00:21:36,570
when we have done surveys of faculty

463
00:21:36,570 --> 00:21:38,748
across the California Community College system,

464
00:21:38,748 --> 00:21:41,370
there may be some response bias

465
00:21:41,370 --> 00:21:44,160
in who's responding to those surveys,

466
00:21:44,160 --> 00:21:46,200
but it seems like the majority of folks

467
00:21:46,200 --> 00:21:47,940
are sort of in the open to, you know,

468
00:21:47,940 --> 00:21:50,640
somewhat moderated use of AI.

469
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:52,320
And students need to know that

470
00:21:52,320 --> 00:21:54,570
because they can get shut down.

471
00:21:54,570 --> 00:21:58,710
When we listen to students' experiences, like for instance,

472
00:21:58,710 --> 00:22:02,010
around the Nectar AI pilot, some students are very reluctant

473
00:22:02,010 --> 00:22:05,670
to engage with an AI tool that instructor's

474
00:22:05,670 --> 00:22:06,870
are encouraging them to use

475
00:22:06,870 --> 00:22:09,960
because they've been told that using AI is cheating.

476
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:12,690
And we're just not being clear enough with our students.

477
00:22:12,690 --> 00:22:14,700
So, again, I think that comes right back

478
00:22:14,700 --> 00:22:16,890
to what is our policy and, you know,

479
00:22:16,890 --> 00:22:19,950
getting to a point where we can agree institutionally

480
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:22,680
what our position is and accepting

481
00:22:22,680 --> 00:22:24,390
the different kind of positionalities

482
00:22:24,390 --> 00:22:26,703
that need to happen vis-a-vis AI use.

483
00:22:27,870 --> 00:22:29,373
But just, again, transparency I think

484
00:22:29,373 --> 00:22:30,600
is a good word for that.

485
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:35,310
Being very transparent about what that use policy is,

486
00:22:35,310 --> 00:22:37,560
class by class, syllabus by syllabus,

487
00:22:37,560 --> 00:22:39,120
but including it in every syllabus

488
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:42,843
would be a great kind of step towards achieving AI literacy.

489
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:49,920
- And since my colleagues on the panel have focused heavily

490
00:22:49,920 --> 00:22:53,700
on thinking of the teaching and learning roles with policy,

491
00:22:53,700 --> 00:22:55,530
I'll step aside for a second

492
00:22:55,530 --> 00:22:57,870
and talk to any administrators in the room,

493
00:22:57,870 --> 00:23:00,600
any technology experts.

494
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:03,240
So, the policies that we have to consider

495
00:23:03,240 --> 00:23:05,340
in addition to academic integrity

496
00:23:05,340 --> 00:23:08,040
and things like that are data governance.

497
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:11,370
This morning we heard Chancellor Christian talk about,

498
00:23:11,370 --> 00:23:15,600
hey, what if we could enable staff to use AI

499
00:23:15,600 --> 00:23:20,070
to streamline workflows regardless of what those are?

500
00:23:20,070 --> 00:23:22,740
And there might be ways to investigate that.

501
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:26,970
But if we just let the horses run wild out of the corral,

502
00:23:26,970 --> 00:23:29,880
does that mean that someone might take data sets

503
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:31,860
that have personally identifiable information

504
00:23:31,860 --> 00:23:33,900
and throw it into something that's gonna train

505
00:23:33,900 --> 00:23:36,270
one of the language learning models?

506
00:23:36,270 --> 00:23:39,420
So we need to be thinking about

507
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:44,420
the different aspects of AI use across the institution

508
00:23:44,790 --> 00:23:49,740
and consider, like, how does this impact student services?

509
00:23:49,740 --> 00:23:53,550
Right now we're about to launch a tool

510
00:23:53,550 --> 00:23:57,030
in the far north region with three rural colleges

511
00:23:57,030 --> 00:24:01,440
to use a multi-channel AI-based platform called Cali

512
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:04,830
to support students as they apply for the college,

513
00:24:04,830 --> 00:24:07,830
as they register for classes, as they fill out the FAFSA,

514
00:24:07,830 --> 00:24:09,870
as they seek basic needs.

515
00:24:09,870 --> 00:24:12,660
And the thing I love about it is

516
00:24:12,660 --> 00:24:15,180
they might have filled out a lot of the forms,

517
00:24:15,180 --> 00:24:17,797
but then it'll prompt them with a text message saying,

518
00:24:17,797 --> 00:24:19,560
"Hey, you haven't registered for classes yet.

519
00:24:19,560 --> 00:24:21,630
Do you wanna do that now? Let me tell you how."

520
00:24:21,630 --> 00:24:25,740
And so to me those are ways to approach

521
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:28,710
when you say how do we approach AI adoption on a campus.

522
00:24:28,710 --> 00:24:31,620
