00:36:18,177 --> 00:36:21,390
the AI Council didn't invent the term AI literacy.

786
00:36:21,390 --> 00:36:23,340
There's a lot of research that had been done

787
00:36:23,340 --> 00:36:25,860
over the years into what that comprises,

788
00:36:25,860 --> 00:36:28,380
you know, from four elements to twenty-four elements.

789
00:36:28,380 --> 00:36:31,290
And, you know, we sort of settled on the six

790
00:36:31,290 --> 00:36:34,140
that are embodied in the HUMANS Principles

791
00:36:34,140 --> 00:36:37,200
as a good kind of distillation of the essence

792
00:36:37,200 --> 00:36:40,713
of the most important elements of AI literacy.

793
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:44,580
One of the things we looked at that was informative,

794
00:36:44,580 --> 00:36:46,650
we had a pretty rich discussion,

795
00:36:46,650 --> 00:36:48,960
was the EU AI Act.

796
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:52,350
And the EU AI Act, there was a similar legislation

797
00:36:52,350 --> 00:36:53,520
that came forward in California

798
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:55,620
and ultimately the governor didn't sign it

799
00:36:56,460 --> 00:36:58,273
because what was similar is it was

800
00:36:58,273 --> 00:37:01,470
a risk-based approach to AI, right?

801
00:37:01,470 --> 00:37:05,040
And, oh no, well, he didn't sign our version

802
00:37:05,040 --> 00:37:06,210
because it wasn't risk-based.

803
00:37:06,210 --> 00:37:08,400
The EU AI Act has a risk-based.

804
00:37:08,400 --> 00:37:10,500
So, it was just sort of, like,

805
00:37:10,500 --> 00:37:12,960
lumped all AI together, the California version.

806
00:37:12,960 --> 00:37:14,670
But if you look at the EU AI Act,

807
00:37:14,670 --> 00:37:17,997
there's this pyramid kind of structure to it.

808
00:37:17,997 --> 00:37:21,150
And at the top is just unacceptable uses of AI.

809
00:37:21,150 --> 00:37:23,370
There's certain stuff you should not,

810
00:37:23,370 --> 00:37:24,990
it is not permissible.

811
00:37:24,990 --> 00:37:29,640
And, you know, no policy should allow, for instance,

812
00:37:29,640 --> 00:37:33,780
using AI to subliminally manipulate people.

813
00:37:33,780 --> 00:37:35,010
That's not okay, right?

814
00:37:35,010 --> 00:37:37,773
Like, we don't support that. That's off limits.

815
00:37:38,820 --> 00:37:43,350
Other uses is kind of scooping up public video,

816
00:37:43,350 --> 00:37:47,040
audio feeds and biometrically processing that

817
00:37:47,040 --> 00:37:48,960
without people's awareness, right?

818
00:37:48,960 --> 00:37:52,440
So, those are kind of the two, like,

819
00:37:52,440 --> 00:37:54,930
signature kind of unacceptable uses.

820
00:37:54,930 --> 00:37:58,200
Right under that there's a high-risk zone

821
00:37:58,200 --> 00:38:02,970
and that's using AI for critical infrastructure.

822
00:38:02,970 --> 00:38:04,830
And high-risk doesn't mean you can't use it,

823
00:38:04,830 --> 00:38:07,080
but it means there needs to be a human in the loop

824
00:38:07,080 --> 00:38:08,250
to break that cycle,

825
00:38:08,250 --> 00:38:12,120
and that there's a rigorous sort of safety protocol in place

826
00:38:12,120 --> 00:38:15,150
to make sure that humans can kind of intercede.

827
00:38:15,150 --> 00:38:17,010
And guess what else is in there?

828
00:38:17,010 --> 00:38:18,423
Educational assessment.

829
00:38:19,350 --> 00:38:21,240
And hiring and employment.

830
00:38:21,240 --> 00:38:24,960
So, educational decisions and hiring decisions.

831
00:38:24,960 --> 00:38:29,010
So, that means we shouldn't just be turning the keys over

832
00:38:29,010 --> 00:38:31,590
to whatever AI has been integrated into Canvas

833
00:38:31,590 --> 00:38:34,243
and say, grade my students and, you know,

834
00:38:34,243 --> 00:38:36,780
I just set that class on autopilot

835
00:38:36,780 --> 00:38:38,100
and I'll check in at the end

836
00:38:38,100 --> 00:38:39,600
when I have to submit the grades, right?

837
00:38:39,600 --> 00:38:42,360
That is not an ethical and appropriate,

838
00:38:42,360 --> 00:38:44,160
so, but it's fine to,

839
00:38:44,160 --> 00:38:46,380
you know, there's a lot of different ways to use,

840
00:38:46,380 --> 00:38:48,180
I'm not saying don't do it, right?

841
00:38:48,180 --> 00:38:49,650
There's a lot of different ways to do that

842
00:38:49,650 --> 00:38:51,573
that would be ethical, transparent.

843
00:38:52,800 --> 00:38:56,013
And, you know, for instance,

844
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:00,570
the ability to have frequent low-stakes assessment,

845
00:39:00,570 --> 00:39:03,360
the AI can empower that in a very helpful

846
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:05,760
and useful way that can promote student learning.

847
00:39:05,760 --> 00:39:07,800
But it needs to be done mindfully, right?

848
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:10,980
In an intentional pedagogical framework

849
00:39:10,980 --> 00:39:13,473
that the instructor is laying out, right?

850
00:39:14,970 --> 00:39:17,340
And that's, I think, we're all in agreement with that.

851
00:39:17,340 --> 00:39:19,740
We're not trying to replace our, you know,

852
00:39:19,740 --> 00:39:23,940
wonderful, magical educational experience

853
00:39:23,940 --> 00:39:28,050
that students have with human instructors with AI.

854
00:39:28,050 --> 00:39:30,330
But there are certainly applications of AI

855
00:39:30,330 --> 00:39:32,940
to that experience that are very helpful

856
00:39:32,940 --> 00:39:37,410
and can allow for more time for deeper conversations

857
00:39:37,410 --> 00:39:42,210
and human interaction that support engagement and learning.

858
00:39:42,210 --> 00:39:44,580
So, I think that would be, you know,

859
00:39:44,580 --> 00:39:46,860
kind of one example that's been helpful

860
00:39:46,860 --> 00:39:49,185
for me to think about what's off limits

861
00:39:49,185 --> 00:39:54,185
and what, you know, needs strong guidelines, and so on.

862
00:39:56,463 --> 00:39:57,450
- I had a couple thoughts.

863
00:39:57,450 --> 00:40:02,450
So, Craig brought up a very specific set of use cases

864
00:40:02,970 --> 00:40:05,340
of things that have been identified,

865
00:40:05,340 --> 00:40:07,890
but we're always gonna be behind the curve, right?

866
00:40:07,890 --> 00:40:09,060
Somebody's gonna think of something

867
00:40:09,060 --> 00:40:10,620
we haven't thought of yet.

868
00:40:10,620 --> 00:40:14,010
And we have policies in place about student behavior,

869
00:40:14,010 --> 00:40:15,630
about faculty and staff behavior.

870
00:40:15,630 --> 00:40:17,580
We have handbooks and all these things.

871
00:40:17,580 --> 00:40:20,160
So, really it boils down to, like,

872
00:40:20,160 --> 00:40:22,170
the culture of the institution.

873
00:40:22,170 --> 00:40:25,710
Are you doing something that you shouldn't be knowingly?

874
00:40:25,710 --> 00:40:28,530
And if you're doing something unwittingly,

875
00:40:28,530 --> 00:40:30,690
then how do we educate you back in the literacy

876
00:40:30,690 --> 00:40:32,790
and fluency conversation.

877
00:40:32,790 --> 00:40:35,310
To your point about things like,

878
00:40:35,310 --> 00:40:39,570
hey, I'd love to use AI to give students feedback,

879
00:40:39,570 --> 00:40:41,580
maybe, you know, it's what I've suggested

880
00:40:41,580 --> 00:40:44,940
to some of the learning management system developers,

881
00:40:44,940 --> 00:40:47,220
rather than making it a teacher-facing tool,

882
00:40:47,220 --> 00:40:48,630
make it a student-facing tool.

883
00:40:48,630 --> 00:40:51,180
In my class, I have always had peer review.

884
00:40:51,180 --> 00:40:52,620
It's a writing intensive class

885
00:40:52,620 --> 00:40:54,570
and I love the revival of rhetoric

886
00:40:54,570 --> 00:40:56,343
because I have to do that, too.

887
00:40:57,360 --> 00:41:02,100
But I also now have students compare AI feedback

888
00:41:02,100 --> 00:41:04,020
to this human student feedback.

889
00:41:04,020 --> 00:41:06,000
And what are the benefits that they get from both?

890
00:41:06,000 --> 00:41:07,740
What is each one missing?

891
00:41:07,740 --> 00:41:10,620
So, they're actually reflecting on what feedback is

892
00:41:10,620 --> 00:41:14,010
and how to generate more valuable feedback for their peers.

893
00:41:14,010 --> 00:41:16,440
So, there are ways that we as educators

894
00:41:16,440 --> 00:41:19,350
can be thinking about the implications of AI.

895
00:41:19,350 --> 00:41:22,530
And again, it's being intentional about it rather than,

896
00:41:22,530 --> 00:41:23,363
hey, there's this tool,

897
00:41:23,363 --> 00:41:25,620
I'm gonna press a button and trust the results.

898
00:41:25,620 --> 00:41:28,860
What is the O, like, observe and verify,

899
00:41:28,860 --> 00:41:29,693
or something like that?

900
00:41:29,693 --> 00:41:33,093
So, I'm gonna get these acronyms if it kills me.

901
00:41:34,178 --> 00:41:35,297
- There's a lot to it.

902
00:41:35,297 --> 00:41:36,930
- And in the spirit of, like,

903
00:41:36,930 --> 00:41:39,690
modify them for your own college culture,

904
00:41:39,690 --> 00:41:43,440
I suggest nobody turn HUMANS into ROBOTS.

905
00:41:43,440 --> 00:41:45,859
Because that's also six letters and probably not-

906
00:41:45,859 --> 00:41:48,060
(audience and panel laughing)

907
00:41:48,060 --> 00:41:49,110
But you get my point, right?

908
00:41:49,110 --> 00:41:52,860
So, the same way we can future-proof policies,

909
00:41:52,860 --> 00:41:54,750
we can also future-proof our approaches

910
00:41:54,750 --> 00:41:57,630
by thinking kind of at a deeper level.

911
00:41:57,630 --> 00:41:59,370
How are we asking students

912
00:41:59,370 --> 00:42:02,070
to behave in our campus community?

913
00:42:02,070 --> 00:42:05,100
How do we take less of a risk-based approach

914
00:42:05,100 --> 00:42:07,020
and more of an asset-based approach and say,

915
00:42:07,020 --> 00:42:08,340
hey, you're bringing your strengths.

916
00:42:08,340 --> 00:42:10,620
We want you to capitalize on those

917
00:42:10,620 --> 00:42:14,010
and use them in ways that are appropriate.

918
00:42:14,010 --> 00:42:16,770
And we're gonna have open conversations about it.

919
00:42:16,770 --> 00:42:19,350
So, to your point about grading again,

920
00:42:19,350 --> 00:42:22,860
I had a conversation with my students in an online class,

921
00:42:22,860 --> 00:42:24,420
what do you all think about AI

922
00:42:24,420 --> 00:42:26,070
in the teaching and learning context?

923
00:42:26,070 --> 00:42:30,667
And the highest liked responses were,

924
00:42:30,667 --> 00:42:32,790
"I don't like it when other students use AI

925
00:42:32,790 --> 00:42:33,623
to write their papers

926
00:42:33,623 --> 00:42:36,330
because I took the time to write mine."

927
00:42:36,330 --> 00:42:38,580
And, "I don't like it when teachers use AI

928
00:42:38,580 --> 00:42:41,550
to grade my papers because I'm paying tuition

929
00:42:41,550 --> 00:42:45,060
and I want to get the value of the expert in the room

930
00:42:45,060 --> 00:42:47,700
who I chose as my instructor."

931
00:42:47,700 --> 00:42:50,220
And so the students, you know,

932
00:42:50,220 --> 00:42:55,220
I know there's a lot of concern about what could go wrong,

933
00:42:55,230 --> 00:42:57,240
but the students are aware of those things as well

934
00:42:57,240 --> 00:42:59,490
and are thinking intelligently about it.

935
00:42:59,490 --> 00:43:02,280
And so if we just have candid conversations with them

936
00:43:02,280 --> 00:43:03,690
about what's appropriate,

937
00:43:03,690 --> 00:43:05,970
I think we can not worry so much about,

938
00:43:05,970 --> 00:43:08,730
let's make a list of things that are not approved

939
00:43:08,730 --> 00:43:13,050
and more, let's all work together to get this done.

940
00:43:13,050 --> 00:43:15,060
- Building a culture of trust.

941
00:43:15,060 --> 00:43:16,950
- Yeah, TRUST is the acronym.

942
00:43:16,950 --> 00:43:20,070
- Yes, happy to follow up those great responses

943
00:43:20,070 --> 00:43:22,770
with a single sort of idea that I've come to.

944
00:43:22,770 --> 00:43:25,950
It's that the only thing that is not allowed in my class

945
00:43:25,950 --> 00:43:27,960
as far as AI is relegating your thinking

946
00:43:27,960 --> 00:43:29,523
or your agency to the machine.

947
00:43:30,780 --> 00:43:31,830
That's it.

948
00:43:31,830 --> 00:43:33,720
So, let me actually go through my AI policy

949
00:43:33,720 --> 00:43:35,400
so you understand exactly why, though,

950
00:43:35,400 --> 00:43:37,110
it's sort of built into the policy itself

951
00:43:37,110 --> 00:43:39,390
and you can see how each one of these three bullet points

952
00:43:39,390 --> 00:43:41,100
addresses that very fact.

953
00:43:41,100 --> 00:43:42,300
So number one, if you provide,

954
00:43:42,300 --> 00:43:45,630
well, first AI is one of the emerging Web3 tools

955
00:43:45,630 --> 00:43:47,520
that promises to shake up both academia

956
00:43:47,520 --> 00:43:49,200
and the professional landscape.

957
00:43:49,200 --> 00:43:50,610
Regardless of how you feel about it,

958
00:43:50,610 --> 00:43:53,580
learning to use it well is an emerging and valuable skill.

959
00:43:53,580 --> 00:43:55,200
Let's start there. Right?

960
00:43:55,200 --> 00:43:56,670
That being said, you can expect to use AI in my classes,

961
00:43:56,670 --> 00:43:59,160
ChatGPT, Midjourney, at a minimum.

962
00:43:59,160 --> 00:43:59,993
Now, please be aware,

963
00:43:59,993 --> 00:44:02,250
one, if you provide minimum prompts,

964
00:44:02,250 --> 00:44:03,750
you will get low-quality results.

965
00:44:03,750 --> 00:44:05,460
You'll need to refine your prompts

966
00:44:05,460 --> 00:44:06,660
in order to achieve better outcomes.

967
00:44:06,660 --> 00:44:07,860
This will take work.

968
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:10,050
So, again, weak outputs, you gotta work at them, right?

969
00:44:10,050 --> 00:44:11,730
We've heard a lot of that actually today

970
00:44:11,730 --> 00:44:15,090
about how prompting and refining is a good thing to teach.

971
00:44:15,090 --> 00:44:16,950
Number two, don't trust anything.

972
00:44:16,950 --> 00:44:19,530
AI, in this case ChatGPT, says,

973
00:44:19,530 --> 00:44:21,270
assume facts it gives you a wrong,

974
00:44:21,270 --> 00:44:22,380
unless you know the answer

975
00:44:22,380 --> 00:44:24,210
or can check it with another source.

976
00:44:24,210 --> 00:44:27,570
You will be responsible for any errors

977
00:44:27,570 --> 00:44:30,720
or omissions provided by the tool.

978
00:44:30,720 --> 00:44:33,450
It works best for topics you understand.

979
00:44:33,450 --> 00:44:37,170
And three, any use of AI tools must be acknowledged,

980
00:44:37,170 --> 00:44:38,850
going back to the transparency.

981
00:44:38,850 --> 00:44:40,710
Please be sure to include a paragraph

982
00:44:40,710 --> 00:44:43,080
at the end of any assignment that uses AI

983
00:44:43,080 --> 00:44:44,517
explaining what you used it for

984
00:44:44,517 --> 00:44:47,460
and the specific prompts you used to get your results.

985
00:44:47,460 --> 00:44:49,230
Failure to do so is in violation

986
00:44:49,230 --> 00:44:51,390
of academic honesty policies.

987
00:44:51,390 --> 00:44:52,680
So, the idea there was,

988
00:44:52,680 --> 00:44:55,500
I heard that now we have the MLA and APA

989
00:44:55,500 --> 00:44:58,650
giving us citation methodologies, which is great.

990
00:44:58,650 --> 00:45:00,810
At the time there wasn't any, so it was like,

991
00:45:00,810 --> 00:45:03,450
how about you just cite your inputs or your outputs

992
00:45:03,450 --> 00:45:05,070
as part of your work cited

993
00:45:05,070 --> 00:45:07,140
because now you know you can export those.

994
00:45:07,140 --> 00:45:10,620
So, give me the input and the output that you used,

995
00:45:10,620 --> 00:45:12,990
add that as an addendum to your work,

996
00:45:12,990 --> 00:45:14,511
and we're good to go.

997
00:45:14,511 --> 00:45:17,130
There is inherent trust there.

998
00:45:17,130 --> 00:45:19,290
So, do not relegate your thinking to the machine

999
00:45:19,290 --> 00:45:21,600
is all I would say, agentic or not.

1000
00:45:21,600 --> 00:45:25,140
- I love that policy. Do you mind if we steal it?

1001
00:45:25,140 --> 00:45:27,720
- It's in the list of the,

1002
00:45:27,720 --> 00:45:28,553
who put this together?

1003
00:45:28,553 --> 00:45:30,060
- Lance Eaton. - Lance Eaton's list.

1004
00:45:30,060 --> 00:45:31,890
It's number 76 if you wanna find mine.

1005
00:45:31,890 --> 00:45:34,440
- Yeah, I don't know if if you all know about it,

1006
00:45:34,440 --> 00:45:37,710
but Lance Eaton has compiled policies

1007
00:45:37,710 --> 00:45:39,390
from faculty all over the,

1008
00:45:39,390 --> 00:45:40,819
is it just the country or the world?

1009
00:45:40,819 --> 00:45:43,020
- Everywhere. It's everywhere.

1010
00:45:43,020 --> 00:45:44,700
- And how many are in there now?

1011
00:45:44,700 --> 00:45:47,730
- There's at least 150 or something.

1012
00:45:47,730 --> 00:45:48,563
130.

1013
00:45:48,563 --> 00:45:50,454
- There's some great examples in there

1014
00:45:50,454 --> 00:45:52,380
from all different disciplines.

1015
00:45:52,380 --> 00:45:55,140
And so if you're looking for inspiration

1016
00:45:55,140 --> 00:45:57,147
for your own policy.

1017
00:45:57,147 --> 00:45:59,250
That that was a great policy.

1018
00:45:59,250 --> 00:46:02,250
Okay, so, what do you guys think is best,

1019
00:46:02,250 --> 00:46:04,440
if we make closing statements and then take questions,

1020
00:46:04,440 --> 00:46:06,209
or take questions and then make closing statements?

1021
00:46:06,209 --> 00:46:08,670
- Questions. - Questions first?

1022
00:46:08,670 --> 00:46:11,340
Okay. There's some hands up.

1023
00:46:11,340 --> 00:46:14,103
Okay, I'm gonna get up here and steal somebody's mic.

1024
00:46:15,600 --> 00:46:16,950
Thank you, Kevin. Is it on?

1025
00:46:20,133 --> 00:46:22,923
Okay, where is a hand?

1026
00:46:27,660 --> 00:46:29,550
- [Kathy] Man, I have so much to say after all that,

1027
00:46:29,550 --> 00:46:31,440
but I will limit what I have to say.

1028
00:46:31,440 --> 00:46:33,750
I'm with you. I'm an English professor.

1029
00:46:33,750 --> 00:46:36,690
And I also started adapting it in my courses

1030
00:46:36,690 --> 00:46:38,583
pretty quickly after 2022.

1031
00:46:39,930 --> 00:46:44,640
But I'm also helping to craft our AI policies on our campus.

1032
00:46:44,640 --> 00:46:47,400
And I use PlayLab in my classes.

1033
00:46:47,400 --> 00:46:50,430
And recently, in my last few semesters,

1034
00:46:50,430 --> 00:46:53,310
I spend time on AI literacy,

1035
00:46:53,310 --> 00:46:54,780
even though it's an English course,

1036
00:46:54,780 --> 00:46:56,370
and about prompting.

1037
00:46:56,370 --> 00:46:58,560
And they took, I asked them after,

1038
00:46:58,560 --> 00:47:03,560
I assessed whatever they thought of that AI literacy bit

1039
00:47:04,590 --> 00:47:07,500
that I gave them and they took away,

1040
00:47:07,500 --> 00:47:09,900
which I thought was kind of impressive,

1041
00:47:09,900 --> 00:47:12,000
that their critical thinking skills,

1042
00:47:12,000 --> 00:47:13,830
who they are, is the most important skill

1043
00:47:13,830 --> 00:47:17,910
they don't wanna lose to ChatGPT, or whomever or whatever.

1044
00:47:17,910 --> 00:47:20,370
So, that's good. So I'm a proponent.

1045
00:47:20,370 --> 00:47:22,710
And I'm trying to work with my faculty

1046
00:47:22,710 --> 00:47:25,350
and train my students and et cetera.

1047
00:47:25,350 --> 00:47:29,640
But the one thing that I am concerned about is,

1048
00:47:29,640 --> 00:47:33,210
earlier you mentioned about the models sounding

1049
00:47:33,210 --> 00:47:35,580
like a white male, right?

1050
00:47:35,580 --> 00:47:39,527
And I do spend some time in my AI literacy portion

1051
00:47:41,340 --> 00:47:43,260
about, not to trust the machine,

1052
00:47:43,260 --> 00:47:45,060
to verify, check, et cetera.

1053
00:47:45,060 --> 00:47:47,767
And I show a little clip about

1054
00:47:47,767 --> 00:47:52,767
how really it can cross lines

1055
00:47:53,910 --> 00:47:56,970
in terms of not representing everybody,

1056
00:47:56,970 --> 00:48:00,690
especially certain groups that are underrepresented.

1057
00:48:00,690 --> 00:48:03,390
So my concern is, as we move forward,

1058
00:48:03,390 --> 00:48:06,900
and we're making these deals with the vendors,

1059
00:48:06,900 --> 00:48:09,390
and it's great, we have all these tools and everything,

1060
00:48:09,390 --> 00:48:12,600
I feel like we're piling on top of everything

1061
00:48:12,600 --> 00:48:14,370
all these wonderful tools, yes,

1062
00:48:14,370 --> 00:48:18,150
but on the baseline foundational model.

1063
00:48:18,150 --> 00:48:20,430
And are we ever gonna go backwards

1064
00:48:20,430 --> 00:48:22,680
and go back to the training part of it

1065
00:48:22,680 --> 00:48:25,770
and try to make that more diverse?

1066
00:48:25,770 --> 00:48:27,870
I don't know the answer, I'm just curious.

1067
00:48:32,208 --> 00:48:33,510
- I would say that one of the ways

1068
00:48:33,510 --> 00:48:35,970
that you can deal with some of the bias,

1069
00:48:35,970 --> 00:48:38,250
because of course we know AI, how it works, right?

1070
00:48:38,250 --> 00:48:40,530
It works off of the data sets that you feed it,

1071
00:48:40,530 --> 00:48:43,080
is by being very intentional with your prompting.

1072
00:48:43,080 --> 00:48:46,230
So, one example that I can use from the visual space mainly

1073
00:48:46,230 --> 00:48:48,540
is I have a lot of caricatures of myself

1074
00:48:48,540 --> 00:48:51,150
that I make for my professional development.

1075
00:48:51,150 --> 00:48:52,860
So, if you see one of my professional development,

1076
00:48:52,860 --> 00:48:54,960
you'll see a little avatar of me wearing a jacket.

1077
00:48:54,960 --> 00:48:57,630
Anyway, to make that,

1078
00:48:57,630 --> 00:49:01,050
I didn't want to insert a picture of myself,

1079
00:49:01,050 --> 00:49:02,340
upload a picture of myself.

1080
00:49:02,340 --> 00:49:04,286
It was early on in the conversation where I was like,

1081
00:49:04,286 --> 00:49:06,060
I don't know if I want to upload personal things,

1082
00:49:06,060 --> 00:49:08,190
personal names, personal anything.

1083
00:49:08,190 --> 00:49:10,230
So, I said, give me a picture

1084
00:49:10,230 --> 00:49:12,720
of a professor teaching a class.

1085
00:49:12,720 --> 00:49:13,970
Well, that's too general.

1086
00:49:14,820 --> 00:49:16,650
Then I said, give me a picture of a professor

1087
00:49:16,650 --> 00:49:21,393
with a beard, glasses, brown skin.

1088
00:49:22,500 --> 00:49:23,333
Nailed it.

1089
00:49:24,900 --> 00:49:26,130
If you think of it that way, then again,

1090
00:49:26,130 --> 00:49:27,210
you can control the outputs

1091
00:49:27,210 --> 00:49:29,100
because you're aware of how it works.

1092
00:49:29,100 --> 00:49:31,500
It generalizes knowledge in a very specific way,

1093
00:49:31,500 --> 00:49:33,510
so you can ask for more specific outputs

1094
00:49:33,510 --> 00:49:35,880
is how I sort of turn to that.

1095
00:49:35,880 --> 00:49:38,550
You can't avoid going back to train the initial model

1096
00:49:38,550 --> 00:49:40,470
to have this data or whatever,

1097
00:49:40,470 --> 00:49:43,230
but you can work with smaller dataset models,

1098
00:49:43,230 --> 00:49:44,520
like NotebookLM.

1099
00:49:44,520 --> 00:49:46,530
You can create your own open source ones

1100
00:49:46,530 --> 00:49:48,960
that can have limited knowledge.

1101
00:49:48,960 --> 00:49:51,150
You can do things sort of to circumvent that.

1102
00:49:51,150 --> 00:49:52,290
But it's a very big issue.

1103
00:49:52,290 --> 00:49:54,630
And I think really having that exact conversation

1104
00:49:54,630 --> 00:49:56,343
with them is the best thing.

1105
00:49:57,510 --> 00:49:59,340
- And if I can tack onto that,

1106
00:49:59,340 --> 00:50:02,040
so, I think that it becomes part of

1107
00:50:02,040 --> 00:50:04,740
the AI literacy conversation and fluency

1108
00:50:04,740 --> 00:50:07,650
when we're supporting students,

1109
00:50:07,650 --> 00:50:10,710
even if we could, and it's a great idea,

1110
00:50:10,710 --> 00:50:12,540
to have the California Community Colleges create

1111
00:50:12,540 --> 00:50:15,330
their own LLM, other institutions are doing it,

1112
00:50:15,330 --> 00:50:19,200
A, I'm pretty sure, and Craig will check me if I'm wrong,

1113
00:50:19,200 --> 00:50:20,640
that the Google agreement

1114
00:50:20,640 --> 00:50:24,270
is in enterprise-level data protections

1115
00:50:24,270 --> 00:50:27,150
and it's not gonna be used to train any models.

1116
00:50:27,150 --> 00:50:28,980
But it does mean that we're using

1117
00:50:28,980 --> 00:50:30,390
the one that they have now,

1118
00:50:30,390 --> 00:50:34,200
and so we need to train students to use the models

1119
00:50:34,200 --> 00:50:35,460
not only that they're gonna use

1120
00:50:35,460 --> 00:50:37,230
when they're at our institutions,

1121
00:50:37,230 --> 00:50:39,660
but that they're gonna use when they're out in the workforce

1122
00:50:39,660 --> 00:50:43,290
because they're going to encounter AI

1123
00:50:43,290 --> 00:50:45,900
that's biased in some way, shape, or form.

1124
00:50:45,900 --> 00:50:48,180
And we want them to be able to know what to do with that.

1125
00:50:48,180 --> 00:50:49,590
And it gets back to the verify,

1126
00:50:49,590 --> 00:50:53,340
but also in the humans, it's the algorithmic bias.

1127
00:50:53,340 --> 00:50:56,850
How are we telling our students to consider that

1128
00:50:56,850 --> 00:51:00,570
when they look at the results, and make adjustments for it

1129
00:51:00,570 --> 00:51:02,610
so that they're producing something

1130
00:51:02,610 --> 00:51:06,570
that they believe represents them and their communities

1131
00:51:06,570 --> 00:51:09,090
and everybody else that they believe

1132
00:51:09,090 --> 00:51:10,503
is part of their network.

1133
00:51:14,400 --> 00:51:16,620
- Yeah, and sorry, what college are you?

1134
00:51:16,620 --> 00:51:18,720
- Napa Valley College. - Napa Valley College. Okay.

1135
00:51:18,720 --> 00:51:20,700
And what's your name? - Kathy.

1136
00:51:20,700 --> 00:51:22,830
- Kathy. Thanks.

1137
00:51:22,830 --> 00:51:27,390
And so, Kathy, I think Rodrigo said,

1138
00:51:27,390 --> 00:51:28,980
you mentioned NotebookLM,

1139
00:51:28,980 --> 00:51:30,660
and one of the things you could do

1140
00:51:30,660 --> 00:51:33,900
is to basically call it a RAG plus LLM,

1141
00:51:33,900 --> 00:51:35,880
which is retrieval augmented generation,

1142
00:51:35,880 --> 00:51:38,130
means you upload your own sources to it

1143
00:51:38,130 --> 00:51:41,730
and then you can focus the attention of the LLM

1144
00:51:41,730 --> 00:51:43,590
on those sources first.

1145
00:51:43,590 --> 00:51:44,423
So, there's different,

1146
00:51:44,423 --> 00:51:48,810
like Notebook LLM lets you do that.

1147
00:51:48,810 --> 00:51:51,360
Nectar AI lets you do that.

1148
00:51:51,360 --> 00:51:55,230
You can create custom GPTs in ChatGPT that let you do that.

1149
00:51:55,230 --> 00:51:58,050
And then that way you can kind of bring,

1150
00:51:58,050 --> 00:52:00,840
you can surface that kind of perspective

1151
00:52:00,840 --> 00:52:03,660
and the voices that you want to make sure

1152
00:52:03,660 --> 00:52:06,150
are represented in the output.

1153
00:52:06,150 --> 00:52:10,050
And so that's a nice kind of, you know,

1154
00:52:10,050 --> 00:52:12,750
tool that you have available to make sure that

1155
00:52:12,750 --> 00:52:14,760
you're kind of surfacing the perspectives

1156
00:52:14,760 --> 00:52:19,760
that need to be included in the conversation with the AI.

1157
00:52:22,470 --> 00:52:24,903
- [Lynn] Okay. We have another question here.

1158
00:52:26,910 --> 00:52:29,520
- [Participant] Thank you. Thank you for this panel.

1159
00:52:29,520 --> 00:52:31,920
I wanted to ask about resistance

1160
00:52:31,920 --> 00:52:36,920
to conceptualizing agency in artificial intelligence.

1161
00:52:37,860 --> 00:52:39,510
In other words, not just skepticism,

1162
00:52:39,510 --> 00:52:44,040
but outright refusal to consider this new technology,

1163
00:52:44,040 --> 00:52:47,223
relatively new technology, as part of their work.

1164
00:52:48,450 --> 00:52:51,810
To frame it, I taught for a number of years

1165
00:52:51,810 --> 00:52:55,290
at a mountain village in Southern Africa

1166
00:52:55,290 --> 00:52:56,580
where we didn't have electricity

1167
00:52:56,580 --> 00:52:58,560
or running water or mimeograph machines.

1168
00:52:58,560 --> 00:53:00,303
Our technology consists of chalk.

1169
00:53:01,140 --> 00:53:04,260
And then to frame it one more further,

1170
00:53:04,260 --> 00:53:07,350
and I'd like your perspectives on this resistance,

1171
00:53:07,350 --> 00:53:10,620
in the Socratic dialogue, writing will produce

1172
00:53:10,620 --> 00:53:13,830
the appearance of wisdom, not true wisdom,

1173
00:53:13,830 --> 00:53:16,620
for they will seem to know many things when they are,

1174
00:53:16,620 --> 00:53:19,770
for the most part, ignorant, since they are not wise,

1175
00:53:19,770 --> 00:53:21,420
but only appear wise.

1176
00:53:21,420 --> 00:53:26,340
So, even writing and printing presses and calculators,

1177
00:53:26,340 --> 00:53:28,680
remember Texas Instruments was

1178
00:53:28,680 --> 00:53:30,720
the death of mathematics, right?

1179
00:53:30,720 --> 00:53:35,430
So, tell me about this outright resistance

1180
00:53:35,430 --> 00:53:38,013
a layer beyond skepticism?

1181
00:53:40,410 --> 00:53:43,590
- So, I think with any technology innovation,

1182
00:53:43,590 --> 00:53:45,720
there's an entire adoption curve,

1183
00:53:45,720 --> 00:53:47,670
if you're familiar with it, by Rogers,

1184
00:53:47,670 --> 00:53:49,410
where you have early adopters,

1185
00:53:49,410 --> 00:53:51,630
a early majority, a late majority,

1186
00:53:51,630 --> 00:53:53,520
and then the tail end,

1187
00:53:53,520 --> 00:53:56,250
which people use sometimes derogatory terms,

1188
00:53:56,250 --> 00:53:57,453
resisters might be one.

1189
00:53:59,520 --> 00:54:03,060
When I first started in faculty development,

1190
00:54:03,060 --> 00:54:07,860
I had an instructor who had a printed out, typewritten sheet

1191
00:54:07,860 --> 00:54:11,280
that students would hand write their attendance.

1192
00:54:11,280 --> 00:54:14,820
And we were talking to him

1193
00:54:14,820 --> 00:54:16,860
about different strategies he could use.

1194
00:54:16,860 --> 00:54:19,530
It would be easier, less work.

1195
00:54:19,530 --> 00:54:22,380
And he's like, "I will never get a computer."

1196
00:54:22,380 --> 00:54:24,360
This is, you know, back when people

1197
00:54:24,360 --> 00:54:26,730
were getting desktops and such.

1198
00:54:26,730 --> 00:54:30,840
And two years after he retired,

1199
00:54:30,840 --> 00:54:33,960
I saw him at the bookstore using his alumni pass

1200
00:54:33,960 --> 00:54:35,760
to get a computer.

1201
00:54:35,760 --> 00:54:39,210
And I said, "What convinced you to finally make the change?"

1202
00:54:39,210 --> 00:54:41,510
And he goes, "My grandson wanted to email me."

1203
00:54:42,450 --> 00:54:46,260
So, it's finding out what's meaningful

1204
00:54:46,260 --> 00:54:48,330
for the people who are resisting

1205
00:54:48,330 --> 00:54:51,480
and what is causing the resistance.

1206
00:54:51,480 --> 00:54:53,010
Like, we said earlier today,

1207
00:54:53,010 --> 00:54:55,230
I really feel like I should have started

1208
00:54:55,230 --> 00:54:57,060
with a psychology degree in this field

1209
00:54:57,060 --> 00:55:01,980
because we need to understand the underlying motivations,

1210
00:55:01,980 --> 00:55:03,870
or lack of motivation,

1211
00:55:03,870 --> 00:55:06,270
for people to at least have conversations.

1212
00:55:06,270 --> 00:55:08,130
We're not asking everybody to do this.

1213
00:55:08,130 --> 00:55:11,400
We're making it opt-in, right?

1214
00:55:11,400 --> 00:55:14,100
At the institution level and at the human level.

1215
00:55:14,100 --> 00:55:17,130
And so those resisters are going to exist.

1216
00:55:17,130 --> 00:55:19,470
We want their voices in the conversation

1217
00:55:19,470 --> 00:55:21,570
if they're willing to participate,

1218
00:55:21,570 --> 00:55:24,990
but at some point, as we've already said,

1219
00:55:24,990 --> 00:55:27,660
it's here and we need to keep moving forward.

1220
00:55:27,660 --> 00:55:31,563
So, we hope you'll join us as kind of an invitation.

1221
00:55:34,350 --> 00:55:36,150
- [Lynn] Pretty much says it, right?

1222
00:55:37,200 --> 00:55:42,200
- I would say, I have a very easy sort of answer to this,

1223
00:55:42,390 --> 00:55:46,290
because I teach writing and rhetoric to my students.

1224
00:55:46,290 --> 00:55:47,340
So, when they are reluctant,

1225
00:55:47,340 --> 00:55:49,650
I'm like, okay, cool.

1226
00:55:49,650 --> 00:55:51,360
Reluct away.

1227
00:55:51,360 --> 00:55:54,960
Because all that means is we're gonna focus on syntax

1228
00:55:54,960 --> 00:55:58,350
and grammar and sentences and complex structures

1229
00:55:58,350 --> 00:55:59,970
and meaning-making,

1230
00:55:59,970 --> 00:56:01,980
and that inherently will make you a good prompter.

1231
00:56:01,980 --> 00:56:03,450
That's a default, though. Don't worry about it, right?

1232
00:56:03,450 --> 00:56:04,800
That's not what we're focusing on.

1233
00:56:04,800 --> 00:56:07,650
We're gonna focus on just composition and communication.

1234
00:56:07,650 --> 00:56:09,720
So, they are reluctant in my class sometimes,

1235
00:56:09,720 --> 00:56:10,950
and despite my policy, I say,

1236
00:56:10,950 --> 00:56:12,840
you don't want to use it, that's okay.

1237
00:56:12,840 --> 00:56:13,673
It's fine.

1238
00:56:13,673 --> 00:56:15,390
But you should know that others will,

1239
00:56:15,390 --> 00:56:17,700
and that there is jobs that are now demanding it,

1240
00:56:17,700 --> 00:56:21,540
and that you are now in this conversation,

1241
00:56:21,540 --> 00:56:23,910
this thing that's here whether you want it to or not,

1242
00:56:23,910 --> 00:56:25,860
and you'd better prepare yourself.

1243
00:56:25,860 --> 00:56:28,413
Because, you know, we've all heard that quote, I'm sure,

1244
00:56:28,413 --> 00:56:31,590
that AI will not replace you, but someone using AI will.

1245
00:56:31,590 --> 00:56:34,890
And I see that happening more and more, just people,

1246
00:56:34,890 --> 00:56:37,230
Fabi earlier was talking about how capitalism

1247
00:56:37,230 --> 00:56:39,930
just keeps putting more work in front of you, right?

1248
00:56:39,930 --> 00:56:43,590
But definitely we can do more work with the help of AI,

1249
00:56:43,590 --> 00:56:45,140
but that shouldn't be the goal.

1250
00:56:48,480 --> 00:56:49,770
- [Steve] Thank you so much for this panel.

1251
00:56:49,770 --> 00:56:52,650
Steve Lisowski, professor of nursing

1252
00:56:52,650 --> 00:56:55,070
at Hartnell Community College.

1253
00:56:55,070 --> 00:56:59,670
I just wanted to share two real quick experiences

1254
00:56:59,670 --> 00:57:02,010
that I had a little bit along the lines

1255
00:57:02,010 --> 00:57:03,090
of what you were talking about

1256
00:57:03,090 --> 00:57:05,670
with administrators and that kind of thing.

1257
00:57:05,670 --> 00:57:08,430
A different perspective that came to me was,

1258
00:57:08,430 --> 00:57:11,910
I'm one of the few people at my college who's using AI,

1259
00:57:11,910 --> 00:57:16,560
and what was interesting was the perspective of the teacher.

1260
00:57:16,560 --> 00:57:19,410
In other words, how it changed my perspective

1261
00:57:19,410 --> 00:57:20,793
about how I taught,

1262
00:57:21,750 --> 00:57:23,370
kind of to what you were saying, Rodrigo,

1263
00:57:23,370 --> 00:57:26,340
about prompts, but not only prompts to the AI,

1264
00:57:26,340 --> 00:57:28,980
but in prompting the AI, how that helped me

1265
00:57:28,980 --> 00:57:31,710
actually engage with the students better

1266
00:57:31,710 --> 00:57:34,560
in terms of discussion and that kind of thing,

1267
00:57:34,560 --> 00:57:38,220
that looking for the quote unquote "right answer."

1268
00:57:38,220 --> 00:57:40,860
And then the other thing that you all spoke of

1269
00:57:40,860 --> 00:57:45,390
was this kind of interesting pervasiveness of,

1270
00:57:45,390 --> 00:57:47,610
I've gone to a couple AI conferences now

1271
00:57:47,610 --> 00:57:50,640
that often focus on the catching the student type thing,

1272
00:57:50,640 --> 00:57:52,890
the cheating, you know, paradigm.

1273
00:57:52,890 --> 00:57:55,620
And I just, I think one of you said it,

1274
00:57:55,620 --> 00:57:56,550
Rodrigo, I think it was you,

1275
00:57:56,550 --> 00:58:01,230
you said that, like, the students are almost more aware

1276
00:58:01,230 --> 00:58:02,850
of the situation than we can be.

1277
00:58:02,850 --> 00:58:06,450
And I had a student who used Grammarly.

1278
00:58:06,450 --> 00:58:10,200
And so she had this amazing paper

1279
00:58:10,200 --> 00:58:15,200
and her conversational tactics were significantly different.

1280
00:58:16,080 --> 00:58:20,100
So, I just mentioned it to her in-person,

1281
00:58:20,100 --> 00:58:22,350
and she said, "Oh, you know, well I use Grammarly."

1282
00:58:22,350 --> 00:58:24,090
And I said, "Oh, okay, well, you know,

1283
00:58:24,090 --> 00:58:27,090
as of right now, we have a policy that we can't use AI."

1284
00:58:27,090 --> 00:58:28,657
So, I said, "Okay, well did you use AI?"

1285
00:58:28,657 --> 00:58:29,610
"No, I used Grammarly."

1286
00:58:29,610 --> 00:58:32,430
And I said, "Well, I think Grammarly now uses AI,"

1287
00:58:32,430 --> 00:58:33,840
you know, kind of thing.

1288
00:58:33,840 --> 00:58:36,300
She was absolutely terrified.

1289
00:58:36,300 --> 00:58:37,680
Like, the concept,

1290
00:58:37,680 --> 00:58:39,930
it wasn't that she was trying to cheat,

1291
00:58:39,930 --> 00:58:41,610
she was literally falling apart

1292
00:58:41,610 --> 00:58:43,530
that she was gonna get dropped from the program.

1293
00:58:43,530 --> 00:58:44,363
And I was like, no, no, no,

1294
00:58:44,363 --> 00:58:45,600
that's not what we're talking about.

1295
00:58:45,600 --> 00:58:49,230
But my point is to what a lot of you have said,

1296
00:58:49,230 --> 00:58:51,030
we bring, like anything,

1297
00:58:51,030 --> 00:58:54,960
a bias to this new technology.

1298
00:58:54,960 --> 00:58:57,660
And I think it's not only important for us

1299
00:58:57,660 --> 00:58:59,250
as we develop these policies,

1300
00:58:59,250 --> 00:59:00,960
we need to be aware of our own biases

1301
00:59:00,960 --> 00:59:02,430
that we're bringing to the table.

1302
00:59:02,430 --> 00:59:03,263
- Absolutely.

1303
00:59:03,263 --> 00:59:04,710
